course: religion-ethics
lesson: 40
title: How to argue: counter-arguments and respect
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A strong thinker looks for the best reasons against their own view, called counter-arguments, and answers them: either by showing why they are weaker, or by changing their mind.
explain: Steel-manning means stating the other side's view in its strongest form before you reply; it is the opposite of making it sound silly, and it is fair to your opponent.
explain: Criticise the idea, never the person: "You only say that because you're a baby" answers nothing, because an attack on someone does not show their reasons are wrong.
explain: Listen to understand, sum up what you heard, look for common ground, keep a calm and polite tone, and accept that thoughtful people may still agree to disagree and stay friends.
explain: For a child today this is how to disagree without a fight, and for a Christian it fits James 1:19: "be quick to listen, slow to speak and slow to become angry"; olympiad judges reward exactly this.
fact: A counter-argument is a reason someone might give against your view.
fact: Strong thinkers look for the best objections to their own view, not the weakest.
fact: You can respond to an objection by showing why it is weaker, or by changing your view.
fact: Changing your mind for a good reason is a strength, and olympiad judges reward it.
fact: Steel-manning means stating the other side's view in its strongest form before replying.
fact: A "straw man" is the opposite: making the other view sound sillier than it is so it is easy to knock down.
fact: A personal attack criticises the person instead of the idea, and it does not answer their reasons.
fact: Active listening means listening to understand, not just waiting to speak, then summing up what you heard.
fact: Agreeing to disagree means accepting that thoughtful people can reach different conclusions.
fact: Disagreement does not have to end a friendship.
fact: Common ground is what both sides agree on; finding it first makes a discussion calmer.
fact: A respectful tone means speaking calmly, using names respectfully and thanking the other team.
fact: The Bible says "be quick to listen, slow to speak and slow to become angry" (James 1:19).
exercise: What is a counter-argument? | A reason against your view | A reason that proves your view is right | A rude remark about the other team | A question the judge asks at the end
exercise: Which objections should a strong thinker look for? | The best and strongest ones against their view | Only the weakest ones, which are easy to beat | None, because looking for them shows weakness | Only the ones that their friends and teammates already agree with
exercise: What is the strongest argument against "We should have no homework"? Pick the best-stated one. | Some practice at home helps you remember what you learned | Homework is good because teachers say so and that is that | People who hate homework are just lazy and want to play | Everybody has always done homework, so it must be good
exercise: Someone says "But that's not fair to the others." Which response is best? | Ask who is treated unfairly and how, then weigh it or change your plan | Say "Fairness doesn't matter" and carry on with your speech anyway | Say "You only care because you are one of the others" and move on | Pretend you did not hear the objection and talk about something else
exercise: Why is it brave to say "You've convinced me"? | It admits a mistake for the truth | It means you have given up and lost the round | It makes the other team feel sorry for you | It is not brave; it shows you are weak
exercise: How do olympiad judges see a team that changes its mind for a good reason? | As a strength, and they reward it | As a weakness, and they take points | As cheating, and they stop the round | As rude, and they give a warning
exercise: What is steel-manning? | Stating the other side's view in its strongest form before replying | Making the other side's view sound as silly as possible so it is easy to beat | Repeating your own view many times until the other side gives up | Refusing to talk about the other side's view at any point at all
exercise: You think pets in school are a bad idea. Which is a steel-man of the other side? | Pets teach children care and responsibility and help some feel calm | Pet lovers just want to play all day and never do any lessons | People who want pets in school have not thought about it at all | Pets in school are a good idea because hamsters and rabbits are very, very cute
exercise: Why does attacking the person not prove them wrong? | It says nothing about whether their reasons are good | It does prove it, if the attack is clever enough | It proves them wrong only if they get upset | It proves them wrong if many people laugh
exercise: Which is a personal attack? | You only say that because you're a baby | I disagree, because it could hurt others | Can you explain what you mean by fair? | That is a good point, but here is another
exercise: What is active listening? | Listening to understand, then summing up what you heard | Waiting quietly until it is your turn to say your piece | Nodding a lot while you plan your own next speech | Listening only to the parts that you already agree with
exercise: Your partner said: "Uniforms save money but some kids find them uncomfortable." Which reply starts with active listening? | So you're saying uniforms save money but can be uncomfortable? | Uniforms are great and I have nothing more to add about it | You always complain about everything, don't you, seriously, every single day | Let's talk about something else, uniforms are boring anyway
exercise: How can you stay friends with someone who disagrees with you? | Respect their reasons and agree to disagree | Keep arguing every day until one of you finally gives in | Stop speaking to them until they come round to your view | Pretend to agree with them and complain about them later
exercise: In a debate about pets in school, what might both sides agree on? | Any animal kept must be well cared for and safe | Pets should be allowed in every room of the school | Children should never learn about animals at all | Teachers should decide everything without asking
exercise: Which is a polite phrase to use when you disagree? | I see your point, but I think differently because… | That is the stupidest thing I have ever heard… | Whatever, you would not understand it anyway… | Nobody cares what you think about this one…
exercise: Why is a respectful tone part of good ethics, not decoration? | It treats the other person as someone who matters | It makes your answer sound longer and cleverer | It is only needed when a judge is in the room | It hides the fact that your own reasons are weak and badly thought out
test: My brother says changing your mind in an argument means you lost. Put him right. | Changing your mind for a better reason is a strength, not losing | Yes, whoever changes their mind first has lost the argument | Yes, a strong thinker never changes their mind about anything | No, changing your mind only counts if a teacher tells you to
test: ur only saying that cos ur a goody-goody church kid. What kind of reply is this? | A personal attack | A strong counter-argument about the reasons given | A clarifying question about what a word means | An example of steel-manning the other side's view
test: Zayd, who is Muslim, thinks differently from you about a case. What is the best way to finish the discussion? | Sum up both views, note what you agree on, and agree to disagree kindly | Keep repeating your view louder until he finally agrees with you | Tell him his religion is the only reason he got the answer wrong in the first place | Walk off and refuse to talk to him again for the rest of the day
test: The other team says: "School should start later because tired children learn worse." You disagree. What should you do first? | Say their point fairly in its strongest form, then reply | Say they just want to stay in bed because they are lazy | Ignore their reason and talk about your own view only | Say "No it shouldn't" and wait for the judges to decide
test: Which is a straw man of "We should have more vegetarian school meals"? | "So you want to ban all meat for ever and force everyone" | "You think more meal choices would help health and animals" | "You want some extra vegetarian options on the menu" | "You think more pupils might enjoy meat-free options"
test: A judge asks: "What is the strongest objection to your answer?" What is the best reply? | Name a real, strong objection and explain how you weigh it | Say there are no objections because your answer is perfect | Name a silly objection that is very easy to knock down | Say the other team's objections are just them being rude
test: oh my god shut up, ur so annoying, u always think ur right. What should you do? | Stay calm, ask what they disagree with, and answer that | Shout back that they are even more annoying than you are, so there | Walk off and tell everyone else how rude they were | Agree with them just so they will stop shouting
test: Ruby argues pets in class are cruel; Ben argues they're good for children. What common ground could they start from? | Both want what is best for the animals and the children | Both think pets should be kept in every single classroom | Both think children should never be near any animals | Both think the head teacher should not have any say
test: You and a friend still disagree after a long talk. What is the wisest thing to think? | Thoughtful people can disagree; the friendship still matters | One of us must be stupid, or we would have agreed by now | The friendship is over, because friends always agree | I must keep arguing tomorrow until they finally give in
test: Which shows active listening? | Summing up what your partner said before you answer | Planning your reply while your partner is still talking | Interrupting as soon as you hear something you dislike | Looking at your notes the whole time your partner speaks
test: After a round, which is the best thing to say to the other team? | Thank you, you made us think hard about this case | We won, so your ideas were obviously all wrong | That was boring and your speaker was really bad | We only lost because the judges liked you more than they liked us
test: James 1:19 says "quick to listen, slow to speak, slow to become angry". Which olympiad habit fits it best? | Active listening and a calm tone before replying | Speaking first and loudest to grab the judges | Getting cross to show you really care a lot | Saying nothing at all so you never make mistakes
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the practice designated "steel-manning"? | The statement of an opposing view in its strongest form prior to reply | The statement of an opposing view in its weakest form prior to reply | The repetition of one's own view until the opponent concedes | The declining of any engagement whatsoever with an opposing view
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a personal attack fails as a rebuttal. | It addresses the person rather than the reasons advanced | It is invariably delivered at insufficient volume | It is permitted solely in the final round of competition | It addresses the reasons rather than the person concerned
exam: Select the response consistent with the established practice of adjudicators in respect of a team which revises its view upon hearing a superior reason. | Such a revision is regarded as a strength and is rewarded | Such a revision is regarded as a defeat and is penalised | Such a revision is regarded as misconduct and disqualified | Such a revision is disregarded as of no consequence
exam: Given that two parties remain in disagreement after reasoned discussion, ascertain the conduct most consistent with the principles herein set out. | To acknowledge the disagreement whilst preserving mutual respect | To persist in argument until one party is compelled to concede | To terminate all further relations between the two parties without delay | To feign agreement whilst disparaging the other party privately
